--TEST--
A reflective write runs the property's `set` hook, the way a write at any other access spelling does — `rule:core-classes/reflect`, `rule:security/reflection-enforces-visibility` and `rule:classes/property-hooks`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:classes/property-hooks`: a hooked property's write is a call to its
// `set`, at every access spelling alike — and inside the hook the property
// names its own backing slot, which is what lets this one transform the value
// it commits without recursing.
class Label {
    public string $shout {
        set(string $v) { $this->shout = $v . "!"; }
    }
    public string $plain;

    public function constructor(string $shout) {
        $this->shout = $shout;
        $this->plain = "as written";
    }
}

// The constructor's own assignment is a write like any other, so the hook has
// already run once by the time anything below reads the slot.
var $label = new Label("start");
echo $label->shout, "\n";

// An ordinary write, for the answer the reflective one has to match.
$label->shout = "here";
echo $label->shout, "\n";

// `rule:core-classes/reflect`: the write through the description reaches the
// same hook. Writing past it would leave the class's own invariant advisory
// for anything holding a description, which is exactly what
// `rule:security/reflection-enforces-visibility` refuses.
var $described = Core\Reflect::forObject($label);
$described->set($label, "shout", "there");
echo $label->shout, "\n";
echo $described->get($label, "shout"), "\n";

// And a property that declares no hook is still written straight to its slot:
// the hook branch is the access spelling that had no class to resolve
// against, not a second way of storing.
$described->set($label, "plain", "rewritten");
echo $label->plain, "\n";
--EXPECT--
start!
here!
there!
there!
rewritten
